SQL Lesson 5: Filter and Sort Your Data
One of the most important SQL skills is also one of the simplest:

Getting only the data you actually need.

WHERE filters the rows.
ORDER BY controls how the results are sorted.

SELECT
    reservation_id,
    hotel_name,
    room_revenue
FROM reservations
WHERE booking_status = 'Confirmed'
  AND room_revenue > 1000
ORDER BY room_revenue DESC;

In this example, we’re looking only at confirmed, higher-value reservations, then ranking them from highest to lowest room revenue.

💡 Concept tip: Your WHERE clause should reflect the business question you’re trying to answer.

⚠️ Include the wrong rows, and even technically correct #SQL can produce a misleading business result.

